<helpdocument version="1.0">
<meta>
<topic id="textscalc01func_skewpxml" indexer="include" status="PUBLISH">
<title id="tit" xml-lang="en-US">SKEWP</title>
<filename>/text/scalc/01/func_skewp.xhp</filename>
</topic>
</meta>
<body>
<bookmark xml-lang="en-US" branch="index" id="bm_id3160101">
<bookmark_value>SKEWP function</bookmark_value>
<bookmark_value>skewness;population</bookmark_value>
<bookmark_value>population skewness</bookmark_value>
</bookmark>
<bookmark xml-lang="en-US" branch="hid/SC_HID_FUNC_SKEWP" id="bm_id3160102" localize="false"/>
<paragraph role="heading" id="hd_id3160101" xml-lang="en-US" level="2" l10n="U"><variable id="skewp"><link href="text/scalc/01/func_skewp.xhp">SKEWP</link>
</variable></paragraph>
<paragraph role="paragraph" id="par_id3160103" xml-lang="en-US" l10n="U"><ahelp hid="HID_FUNC_SKEWP">Returns the skewness of a distribution using the population standard deviation.</ahelp> Skewness characterizes the degree of asymmetry of a distribution around its mean.</paragraph>
<paragraph role="heading" id="hd_id3160104" xml-lang="en-US" level="3" l10n="U">Syntax</paragraph>
<paragraph role="code" id="par_id3160105" xml-lang="en-US" l10n="U">SKEWP(Number1; Number2; ...Number30)</paragraph>
<paragraph role="paragraph" id="par_id3160106" xml-lang="en-US" l10n="U"><emph>Number1, Number2, ... Number30</emph> are up to 30 numerical values or ranges representing the entire population.</paragraph>
<paragraph role="heading" id="hd_id3160107" xml-lang="en-US" level="3" l10n="U">Remarks</paragraph>
<paragraph role="paragraph" id="par_id3160108" xml-lang="en-US" l10n="U">SKEWP calculates <emph>population</emph> skewness, using the population standard deviation (dividing by N). Use <link href="text/scalc/01/04060185.xhp#skew" name="SKEW">SKEW</link> for <emph>sample</emph> skewness, which uses the sample standard deviation (dividing by N-1).</paragraph>
<paragraph role="paragraph" id="par_id3160109" xml-lang="en-US" l10n="U">Text and empty cells within referenced ranges are skipped. Errors encountered during reference iteration propagate and cause the function to return an error. Scalar text arguments cause an error (Err:502). At least one numeric value is required.</paragraph>
<paragraph role="paragraph" id="par_id3160110" xml-lang="en-US" l10n="U">If the population variance is zero (for example, all values are identical, or only one value is given), the result is undefined and the function returns an error.</paragraph>
<paragraph role="heading" id="hd_id3160111" xml-lang="en-US" level="3" l10n="U">Example</paragraph>
<paragraph role="paragraph" id="par_id3160112" xml-lang="en-US" l10n="U"><item type="input">=SKEWP(1; 2; 3; 4; 5)</item> returns approximately <item type="input">0</item> because the distribution is symmetric.</paragraph>
<paragraph role="paragraph" id="par_id3160113" xml-lang="en-US" l10n="U"><item type="input">=SKEWP(1; 1; 1; 2; 3)</item> returns a positive value because the distribution has a longer tail toward higher values.</paragraph>
<embed href="text/shared/00/00000001.xhp#siehe"/>
<paragraph role="paragraph" id="par_id3160114" xml-lang="en-US" l10n="U"><link href="text/scalc/01/04060185.xhp#skew" name="SKEW">SKEW</link>.</paragraph>
</body>
